The area swept out by a line between the pulsar and the center of mass from point P{}_{1} to P{}_{2} is calculated by

\\Delta S_{\\rm pulsar}=\\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-\\theta_{1}}^{+\\theta_{2}}r^{2}d\\theta =\\int_{0}^{\\theta_{2}}\\left[\\frac{a(1-e^{2})}{1+e\\cos\\theta}\\right]^{2}d\\theta,  where \\theta_{1} (or \\theta_{2}) is the angle between the x-axis and the line OP{}_{1} (or OP{}_{2}), \\theta_{1}=\\theta_{2}=\\pi-\\arctan(y_{\\rm star}/x_{\\rm star}). The total area enclosed by the pulsar's elliptical orbit is S_{\\rm pulsar}=\\pi(1-e^{2})^{1/2}a^{2}. According to Kepler's second law, the ratio of these two areas is equal to the duration of the active time window (\\Delta P_{\\rm orb}=4\\,\\rm days), in which the pulsar moves from point P{}_{1} to P{}_{2}, divided by P_{\\rm orb}. This means the duty cycle

